# Ridgemere, more of its own: the four Houses and their colours, the Council of Houses in the leaking Rain Hall, the
# Watch, the Sump and its stairs, the wharf and the Greatly Yard, the smokehouses and the Fish Hall, the Sound, the
# terraces up to the Heights, and the rain that never stops.

scene rg_rm_colours
when topic=lore|smalltalk region=ridgemere
a : Greatly blue, Grayrock grey, Fell green, Mossgrave brown. My pup learned them all today. | Saw two House liveries cross the street without a nod. | Greatly servants wear blue even to funerals.
b[noble] : The colours are older than the city walls. Wear them with care.
b[servant] : Wear the wrong colour in the wrong street and you'll know it.
b : Clever pup. | Feud's on again, then. | Blue suits them.
a : Cleverer than me. | When isn't it? | It does.

scene rg_rm_grayrock_quarry
when topic=lore|work region=ridgemere
a : Grayrock's quarry face in the Rainward Hills came down after the rain. | Half the city's built from Grayrock stone. | They're cutting a new face at the quarry. You can hear the wedges from the North Gate.
b[miner] : Wet stone splits wrong. They should wait. They never wait.
b[noble] : House Grayrock built Ridgemere. The city should remember it.
b : Anyone under it? | And the other half's leaking. | All day and night.
a : Two, they say. | Most of it. | All day.

scene rg_rm_fell_timber
when topic=lore|work region=ridgemere
a : Fell's log rafts came down the Rainrun this morning. | The Fell Sawmill's working by lamplight now. | House Fell owns every cedar in the Rainwood.
b[laborer] : I rafted for Fell one season. Lost two toes to the cold.
b[crafter] : Fell timber's the best there is. Pricey, but straight.
b : Big ones? | They must have an order. | And charges for the shade.
a : Big as towers. | The Greatly Yard's, I'd bet. | For the shade, yes.

scene rg_rm_glassworks
when topic=lore region=ridgemere
a : The glassworks fires never go out, they say. | My neighbour got a pane of glass from the works. Wouldn't let anyone touch it. | Smoke from the glassworks is thick today.
b[crafter] : Glass is sand and patience and fire. Mostly fire.
b[old] : Glass once only went into the Rain Hall. Now merchants have it.
b : Hot work. | Proud of it, I'd wager. | Thicker than the fog.
a : The hottest. | Very proud. | Thicker.

scene rg_rm_greatly_launch
when topic=lore|smalltalk region=ridgemere
a : The Greatly Yard's launching the big hull at high tide tomorrow. | The whole wharf turned out to watch the launch. | Greatly named their new ship after Lady Celestine. Of course they did.
b[sailor] : Launch at high tide or not at all. She'll float or she won't.
b[noble] : House Greatly knows how to make a show.
b[child] : Can we watch? I want to see the splash!
b : I'll be there. | Grand sight. | Shameless.
a : Come early. | It was. | Shameless and sweet.

scene rg_rm_smokehouses
when topic=lore|work region=ridgemere
a : The smokehouses on the wharf are running night and day. | Everything below the Commons smells of smoked fish. | The smokehouses want more salt than the pans can rake.
b[fisher] : The Fish Hall buys every fish we catch. And pays half what they're worth.
b[cook] : Wharf smoked herring. Best in the world. Don't tell them I said so.
b : Herring's running, then. | Even the washing. | Salt's dear, then.
a : Running thick. | Even the washing. | Very dear.

scene rg_rm_rain_counting
when topic=smalltalk|weather region=ridgemere
a : Forty days of rain and counting. | I saw the sun yesterday, I'm sure of it. | My fur hasn't been dry since the last moon.
b[old] : Forty? I've seen ninety. Rain's Ridgemere's oldest citizen.
b[child] : I like the rain! Puddles everywhere!
b : Forty-one tomorrow. | You were dreaming. | Nobody's has.
a : Probably. | Probably. | Nobody's.

scene rg_rm_sump_stairs
when topic=smalltalk region=ridgemere
a : The Sump stairs flooded to the third step. | Somebody's built a bridge of planks over the worst of the Sump. | Children in the Sump sell rats on sticks now.
b[beggar] : Rats on sticks. Better than nothing on sticks.
b[guard] : We pulled a body from the Sump channel this morning. Don't go there alone.
b : It'll drop by morning. | Good for them. | That's the Sump.
a : Maybe. | Good. | It is.

scene rg_rm_watch_fours
when topic=work region=ridgemere a.role=guard
a : Captain says fours in the Sump now, not pairs. | Lost my lantern chasing a cutpurse through the works. | Watch pay's late again. The Council forgot us.
b[guard] : Fours, and still I don't like it after dark.
b : Safer that way. | Did you catch him? | Of course it is.
a : Slower too. | He went into the Sump. | Of course.

scene rg_rm_council_heads
when topic=lore region=ridgemere
a : Lord Greatly walked the wharf today, umbrella and all. | They say the Council always ends up doing what Greatly wants. | Lady Fell spoke first in the Council this season, they say. First time in years.
b[official] : The Council serves the city, not a House. In principle.
b[noble] : The richest House has the loudest voice. That's what a council is.
b : An umbrella in Ridgemere? Optimist. | Everyone says that. | High time.
a : A dry one. | Everyone. | Long time coming.

scene rg_rm_harbour_dues
when topic=work region=ridgemere a.job=sailor|merchant|fisher|carter
a : The Harbourmaster wants a penny a barrel now. | Dues up again at the Customs House. The Council's roof won't mend itself. | A ship turned back rather than pay the dues.
b[sailor] : Southern ships will put in somewhere cheaper. See if they don't.
b[merchant] : I'll put it on the price. Someone else always pays.
b : Robbery. | Of course they are. | Can't blame them.
a : Daylight robbery. | Of course. | Nor me.

scene rg_rm_sound_fog
when topic=lore|weather region=ridgemere
a : Fog bell at the end of the piers rang all night. | Couldn't see the end of the wharf this morning. | The fog comes up the Sound faster than a wolf can run.
b[sailor] : Fog's the worst. Rain you can sail through.
b[old] : The fog took a pier watchman once. Walked off the end and never came back.
b : I know. I counted the strokes. | Nor the water. | Faster than a horse.
a : Every one. | Nor the ships. | And twice as cold.

scene rg_rm_upper_streets
when topic=smalltalk region=ridgemere
a : The Heights have drains that actually work. | Went up to deliver in the Heights. Clean doorsteps, all of them. | Up the hill, the rain's the same but the roofs don't leak.
b[noble] : Drains cost money. Money comes from work. Work, then.
b[laborer] : Nobody up there's ever carried a sack in their life.
b : Must be nice. | Clean and cold. | Lucky them.
a : Must be. | Very cold. | Lucky.

scene rg_rm_soup_kitchen
when topic=smalltalk region=ridgemere
a : The Soup Kitchen line went round the corner today. | They put barley in the Soup Kitchen soup again. Real barley. | The Houses gave to the Soup Kitchen. Just before the Council vote.
b[priest] : The Bright One feeds the hungry through whoever is willing. Even the Houses.
b[beggar] : Real barley. I had two bowls. Don't tell.
b : Hungry times. | A feast, then. | Funny timing.
a : They are. | Nearly. | Very funny.

scene rg_rm_shipyard
when topic=work region=ridgemere a.job=crafter|laborer|apprentice|sailor
a : Caulked seams all day in the Greatly Yard. My paws are black with pitch. | The Greatly Yard is hiring sawyers. | They laid a new keel this morning. Fell cedar.
b[crafter] : Pitch comes off with butter. Old shipwright's trick.
b : Hard trade. | Good pay? | A big one?
a : Sticky trade. | Fair enough. | Big as a hall.

scene rg_rm_hills_back
when topic=lore region=ridgemere
a : The hill behind the Heights shed a whole slope after the storm. | They say an older city lies under the terraces. | Ridgemere's pressed between the hills and the Sound, my father says.
b[old] : The hills give the stone and take it back. That's the bargain.
b[scholar] : The Hall of Records speaks of an older town. The rest is tavern talk.
b : Mercy. | Tavern talk. | He's right.
a : Mercy. | Probably. | He usually is.

scene rg_rm_mud_carts
when topic=smalltalk region=ridgemere place=street|market|gate
a : A cart sank to its axles on the road to the Commons Market. | Mud's up to my hocks on the wharf lane. | Somebody laid straw in the South Gate road. It's gone already.
b[carter] : Ridgemere mud eats wheels. I carry two spares.
b[child] : I lost a boot! It's still down there somewhere!
b : Every week. | Every day. | Always is.
a : Every week. | Always. | Always.

scene rg_rm_house_feud
when topic=lore region=ridgemere
a : Grayrock and Fell came to blows over a boundary stone, they say. | Greatly won't buy Mossgrave wool for its sailcloth this year. | The feud's been quiet a month. That worries me.
b[noble] : A boundary stone is never only a stone.
b[servant] : We're told not to speak to their servants. We speak anyway.
b : Over a stone? | Petty. | Me too.
a : Over a stone. | Very petty. | Me too.

scene rg_rm_tenements
when topic=smalltalk region=ridgemere place=home|street
a : Twelve of us in two rooms now. | The landlord's patched the roof with sailcloth. | Our tenement stair's rotting from the bottom up.
b[old] : Sleep in turns. That's how we did it.
b : It's always crowded. | That'll hold a week. | Mind the fourth step.
a : Always. | Barely. | I always do.

scene rg_rm_lamp_oil
when topic=smalltalk region=ridgemere time=dusk|night
a : Lamp oil's dearer than ale this month. | We burn fish oil now. Smells like the wharf. | Dark by the fourth hour after noon. I hate the season.
b[merchant] : Wickham's has better oil off the southern ships. Better light, worse price.
b : Everything's dear. | Better than dark. | Long nights.
a : Everything. | Barely. | Very long.

scene rg_rm_lime_kilns
when topic=lore|work region=ridgemere
a : The lime burners came off the kilns today. White to the ears. | Smoke from the lime kilns again, see? | They say the lime burners never wash.
b[laborer] : I burned lime one winter. Your eyes never forgive you.
b : Like ghosts. | Thick today. | Would you, in that?
a : Chalky ghosts. | Very thick. | Probably not.

scene rg_rm_rain_hall_buckets
when topic=lore region=ridgemere
a : They've counted forty buckets in the Rain Hall now. | A councillor's papers got soaked mid-speech. | Someone should pay to mend the Rain Hall roof.
b[official] : Whoever mends the roof would own the Hall. That's the problem.
b[old] : The day the roof stops leaking, the city falls. Leave it.
b : Forty! | Serves him right. | Someone should.
a : Forty. | Ha. | Nobody will.

scene rg_rm_gulls
when topic=smalltalk region=ridgemere place=shore|market|street
a : A gull stole a whole herring off my table. | Gulls are thick on the wharf roofs today. Storm coming. | Gulls are the only fat things in the Sump.
b[fisher] : Gulls know the weather before we do.
b[child] : I'm going to train one! It'll fetch me things!
b : Cheeky birds. | Probably. | True enough.
a : Cheeky thieves. | Probably. | Too true.

scene rg_rm_mourning_bell
when topic=lore region=ridgemere
a : The Chapel of the Tide rang its bell twice this week. | Two boats didn't come back from the Sound. | They're reading names at the Chapel of the Tide again.
b[priest] : The water keeps its dead. We keep their names.
b[sailor] : I knew one of the crews. Good wolves.
b : Hard week on the water. | I'm sorry. | Too many names.
a : Very hard. | So am I. | Far too many.

scene rg_rm_fish_hall_dawn
when topic=smalltalk|work region=ridgemere time=dawn
a : The Fish Hall's loud as a riot this morning. | First boats in before the bell. | Herring cheap at the Sump Market this morning, if you're quick.
b[fisher] : Good catch, cheap price. That's the curse of a good catch.
b[cook] : I'll take the lot if it's fresh.
b : Always is. | Early luck. | I'll be quick.
a : Always. | Early indeed. | Be quick.

scene rg_rm_hungry_winter
when topic=lore region=ridgemere band=friends|family|spouses|acquaintances
a : My grandmother remembers the hungry winter. Ate boiled leather. | They say the Houses kept their stores shut the hungry winter. | Another hungry winter would finish the Sump.
b[old] : I remember it. The Houses ate. We boiled boots.
b[noble] : The Houses opened their stores in the end. People forget that.
b : Mercy. | I believe it. | Let's hope not.
a : Mercy. | So do I. | Let's hope.

scene rg_rm_tarred_roofs
when topic=work region=ridgemere a.job=crafter|laborer|apprentice
a : Spent all day tarring roofs in the drizzle. | The tar won't take on wet tile. | Six roofs this week and every one leaks again.
b[crafter] : Warm the tar, wait for a dry hour. If one comes.
b : Wet work. | It never does. | That's Ridgemere.
a : Very wet. | Never. | That's Ridgemere.

scene rg_rm_southern_sailors
when topic=smalltalk region=ridgemere place=tavern|shore|street
a : Southern sailors singing in the Tarred Rope all night. | A southern sailor sold me a fruit I couldn't name. | The southerners complain about our cold like it's our fault.
b[innkeeper] : They drink, they pay, they sing. I don't mind the singing.
b[sailor] : They'd freeze stiff on a Ridgemere boat. Soft lot.
b : Loud, but cheerful. | Was it good? | It is, a little.
a : Very loud. | Sour. Then sweet. | A little.

scene rg_rm_council_vote
when topic=lore region=ridgemere
a : Council voted three Houses to one on the new harbour wall. | Mossgrave walked out of the Council again. | They say Mossgrave holds the deciding vote this season.
b[official] : Three to one is a majority. Majorities pass.
b[noble] : Walking out is a vote too, in its way.
b : Who lost? | Again? | Lucky Mossgrave.
a : Fell. | Again. | Lucky and loud.

scene rg_rm_snow_rain
when topic=weather|smalltalk region=ridgemere season=winter
a : Snow, then rain on the snow. Grey slush everywhere. | Snow lasted an hour. Now it's rain again. | It's too warm to snow and too cold to bear.
b[old] : Ridgemere never gets a proper winter. Just a long wet one.
b[child] : I made a snow wolf! It melted already.
b : Typical. | Always does. | That's Ridgemere.
a : Typical. | Always. | That's Ridgemere.

scene rg_rm_cutpurses
when topic=smalltalk region=ridgemere place=market|street
a : Lost my purse in the Commons Market crush. Never felt a thing. | Keep your coin in your mouth at the Sump Market. Old Ridgemere habit. | Cutpurses work the Fish Hall at dawn.
b[guard] : They pass it hand to hand. Gone in a breath.
b[beggar] : I never took nothing. Ask anyone.
b : Ridgemere fingers. | Wise. | Every day.
a : Too quick. | Very wise. | Every day.

scene rg_rm_house_weddings
when topic=lore region=ridgemere
a : They say Greatly and Mossgrave are making a match. | A House wedding means a week of free bread at the gates. | The last House wedding ended the Fell feud. For a year.
b[noble] : A marriage between Houses is a treaty with flowers.
b : That'll shift the Council. | Can't wait. | Only a year?
a : It will. | Nor me. | A year.

scene rg_rm_estate_grounds
when topic=lore|smalltalk region=ridgemere
a : Delivered to Mossgrave Court. They've a temple on an island in the pond. | Fellhall's drive is lined with cedars a mile long. | Lady Maren hasn't left Grayrock Hall in years, they say.
b[servant] : The fountains run all day there. In this city. As if we needed more water.
b[noble] : Grounds like that take three generations to grow.
b : An island temple? | I've walked it. | Not once?
a : For the fish, maybe. | Long walk. | Not once.

scene rg_rm_wharf_customs
when topic=work region=ridgemere a.job=official|merchant|sailor
a : The Customs House opened every crate on the southern ship. | The City Office wants a permit for my stall now. | The clerks at the City Office lost my rent receipt. Again.
b[official] : Every crate, every time. That's what customs means.
b[merchant] : A permit, a fee, a stamp. Then they wonder why prices climb.
b : Find anything? | Since when? | Again?
a : Oranges. Lots of oranges. | Since this morning. | Third time this year.
